---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'How DevSnap makes money',
  url: `${SITE.domain}/advertising/`,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: SITE.author },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.publisher },
};
---

<Base
  title="How DevSnap Makes Money — Display Advertising"
  description="DevSnap is funded by display advertising and nothing else. What that means, where the ads sit, and what we do not do."
  canonicalPath="/advertising/"
  schemas={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <ol class="crumbs">
      <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
      <li>Advertising</li>
    </ol>

    <header class="tool-hero">
      <h1>How this site makes money</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        One sentence: display advertising, and nothing else. This page explains
        what that means in practice, because "we make money somehow" is a
        reasonable thing to want to know about a site you are pasting your data
        into.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose mt-3">
      <h2>Where the money comes from</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is funded entirely by display advertising. Google AdSense
        places ads on the pages, and the ad networks pay for the impressions
        and the clicks. The site is free, there is no paid tier and there is
        nothing to buy.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the whole business model. There is no affiliate marketing, no
        lead selling, no data brokerage, and no paid placement in the tool
        results.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not sell, rent or share your data. We do not have your data in the first place.</li>
        <li>We do not build a profile of you across websites. We do not run a behavioural advertising system of our own.</li>
        <li>We do not require an account, and we never ask for an email address.</li>
        <li>We do not place an advert inside a tool result or next to a value you copied. A tool's output is never an advert.</li>
        <li>We do not accept payment to change how a tool behaves or what it reports.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why the tools run in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        The tools process your input on your own machine, and nothing is sent
        to us. This is not a policy promise that could change quietly, it is
        how the site is built: there is no endpoint that receives what you
        paste.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical benefit for you is that a payload containing a customer
        record, a token or a credential stays on your machine. The benefit for
        us is that we do not have to handle other people's secrets, which is
        also why the site is cheap to run.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the ads look the way they do</h2>
      <p>
        The layout is deliberately restrained. Advertisements sit below the
        tool and inside the article, never on top of the input, never between
        the tool and its result, and never styled to look like a button, a
        download link or a navigation item. Each one is labelled as an
        advertisement, and there is never more than one in a single screen.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a tool page does not have enough content to justify an advert, the
        advert is not there. We would rather lose the impression than make the
        page unreadable.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertisers</h2>
      <p>
        Ads are served by Google and its advertising partners. We do not
        control which advertisements appear, we do not endorse the products
        or services in them, and we are not responsible for the content of
        any advertiser's site that you follow from one.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you follow an advert to a site that turns out to be deceptive,
        report it to Google and we will block the advertiser from this site
        where we are able to.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <p>
        You can accept or reject advertising cookies with the banner, and
        change your mind later with the "Cookie choices" link in the footer.
        You can also opt out of personalised advertising at
        <a href="https://adssettings.google.com/" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">adssettings.google.com</a>,
        or block ads entirely with an extension. The tools work exactly the
        same either way.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full detail is in the <a href="/cookies/">Cookie Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Asking us to remove an advert</h2>
      <p>
        If an advert on this site is for something you consider illegal,
        deceptive or simply inappropriate, tell us at
        <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a> with the
        page URL and a description of the advert, and we will block it and
        report it to the network.
      </p>

      <h2>Who writes the content</h2>
      <p>
        The guides and the written notes on each tool are written by
        {SITE.author}. Where a guide covers a specification, the specification
        is the source of truth and the guide links to it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
